I actually tried to spread the pathfinding calls over multiple frames, and I still got terrible performance:
```
public partial class PathTargetScheduler : Node
{
	public static PathTargetScheduler Instance { get; private set; }

	public int MaxTargetsPerPhysicsFrame = 16;

	private readonly Queue<MoveOrder> Pending = new();

	private readonly struct MoveOrder
	{
		public readonly RTSUnitMovement Agent;
		public readonly Vector3 TargetPosition;
		public readonly Vector3 StandingDirection;
		public readonly bool AllowPartialPath;

		public MoveOrder(RTSUnitMovement Agent, Vector3 TargetPosition, Vector3 StandingDirection, bool AllowPartialPath)
		{
			this.Agent = Agent;
			this.TargetPosition = TargetPosition;
			this.StandingDirection = StandingDirection;
			this.AllowPartialPath = AllowPartialPath;
		}
	}

	public override void _EnterTree()
	{
		Instance = this;
	}

	public void Enqueue(RTSUnitMovement Agent, Vector3 TargetPosition, Vector3 StandingDirection, bool AllowPartialPath)
	{
		Pending.Enqueue(new MoveOrder(Agent, TargetPosition, StandingDirection, AllowPartialPath));
	}

	public override void _PhysicsProcess(double Delta)
	{
		for (int i = 0; i < MaxTargetsPerPhysicsFrame && Pending.Count > 0; i++)
		{
			var Order = Pending.Dequeue();

			if (!IsInstanceValid(Order.Agent))
			{
				continue;
			}

			Order.Agent.ApplyMoveOrder(Order.TargetPosition, Order.StandingDirection, Order.AllowPartialPath);
		}
	}
}
```


If just one call for one unit takes ~2ms, then it means processing chunk of merely 10 units in a single frame drops the game below 60FPS, under a very bold assumption that literally nothing else is happening on the game thread.

Let's say we process just 5 unit path queries per frame. If player orders 200 units, it will take 40 frames for all units to even start moving to destination. At 60fps, that's like 0.6-0.7s from player input for order to actually have full effect.

At 60-120APM RTS player pace, this is just not feasible.
